---
import ExerciseLayout from '../layouts/ExerciseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
---
<ExerciseLayout
  title="Hip Thrust 1RM Calculator — Estimate Your Hip Thrust Max"
  description="Estimate your barbell hip thrust one rep max from a set of 1–12 reps. Six formulas with the range between them, what counts as a full rep, and setup tips."
  path="/hip-thrust-calculator/"
  name="Hip Thrust Calculator"
  h1="Hip Thrust One Rep Max (1RM) Calculator"
  lead="Estimate your barbell hip thrust max to set training weights. Hip thrusts are rarely tested as a single, so treat the number as a programming tool rather than a target."
  exercise="hip-thrust"
  lift="Hip thrust"
  example={{ weight: 225, reps: 8, unit: 'lb' }}
  related={[LINKS.squat, LINKS.deadlift, LINKS.percent, LINKS.plates]}
  sources={['lesuer', 'reynolds', 'epley']}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="ht-estimate-heading">
    <h2 id="ht-estimate-heading">How to estimate your hip thrust max</h2>
    <p class="answer">
      Hip thrust a weight you can lift for 5–8 reps that each reach full lockout, and enter the
      weight and reps above. For example, 225 lb × 8 estimates a hip thrust max of roughly
      270–287 lb.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of the six formulas was tested on the hip thrust — the studies we cite used the bench
      press, squat, deadlift and leg press. Hip thrusts are also usually trained with higher reps,
      where every formula is less accurate. Use the estimate to choose working weights, not as an
      exact max.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ht-rep-heading">
    <h2 id="ht-rep-heading">What counts as a full hip thrust rep</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Full lockout at the top:</strong> hips fully extended, with the torso roughly parallel to the floor. Reps that stop short of lockout inflate the estimate.</li>
      <li><strong>Same range every rep:</strong> lower the bar to the same point each time.</li>
      <li><strong>No bouncing</strong> off the floor or the plates between reps.</li>
      <li><strong>Same setup</strong> — bench height, foot position and bar position — as the sets you compare with.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ht-setup-heading">
    <h2 id="ht-setup-heading">Setup changes the number</h2>
    <p>
      Bench height, how far your feet are from your hips, and whether you pause at the top all
      change how hard a hip thrust is. A machine hip thrust or a glute bridge from the floor is a
      different lift again. An estimate only applies to the setup you used, so keep it the same
      when you track progress.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ht-safety-heading">
    <h2 id="ht-safety-heading">Hip thrust safety and comfort</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Brace the bench against a wall or rack so it cannot slide away from you.</li>
      <li>Use a thick bar pad and place the bar over the crease of the hips, not on the stomach.</li>
      <li>Use full-size plates if you can, so the bar is high enough to roll over your legs.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ht-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="ht-faq-heading">Hip thrust max: common questions</h2>
    <h3>How do I calculate my hip thrust max by hand?</h3>
    <p>
      Use the Epley formula: <strong>1RM = weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)</strong>. For 225 lb × 8:
      225 × (1 + 8 ÷ 30) = <strong>285 lb</strong>. The calculator runs five more published
      formulas and shows the range between them.
    </p>
    <h3>Should I test a true 1RM on the hip thrust?</h3>
    <p>
      Usually not. The hip thrust is mostly a training exercise rather than a tested lift, and the
      range of motion tends to shorten with very heavy weights. An estimate from a set of full reps
      is enough to set training percentages.
    </p>
    <h3>Does the estimate work for a hip thrust machine?</h3>
    <p>
      Only for that machine. Machines differ in resistance curves and starting weight, so a machine
      estimate cannot be compared with a barbell hip thrust or with a different machine.
    </p>
  </section>
</ExerciseLayout>
